      "math_academy_id": "q-158435",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Compute $P(1<X<3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{3}+x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}(x-2) & 2<x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{3}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2x]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}([\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}])+\\frac{1}{8}([\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2}-2(3)]-[\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-2(2)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(6-\\frac{3}{4})+\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{9}{2}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{21}{4})+\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{32}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
